Onyx logic is a place where you can  learn by doing  we combine theoretical knowledge and  understanding of biomedical data and  bioinformatics with practical examples  where you can apply what you have  learned and growing your ability to  start thinking creatively develop  independent projects and develop the  skills that you need to really be able  to be proficient with bioinformatics  this can be done in either one of two  ways you can either focus on the  biomedical research to develop research  and curiosity using data analysis  or you can be more focused on developing  coding skills and analytical thinking  these two tracks are divided by the type  of tool that we're going to prioritize  throughout the learning  if we're focused on the biomedical  research side we're going to primarily  rely  on a user-friendly interactive tool  called the t-bion for platform  there's little to no coding involved you  primarily assemble together pipelines or  sequences of steps to understand the  outcomes  on the other hand if you're more focused  on the data science or the skill based  approach you're going to prioritize  learning syntax different packages and  analytical methods implemented in coding  languages like R and python  so as you develop those coding skills  you will also be introduced to different  types of challenges and projects but  primarily that illustrate how specific  challenges are addressed using skills in  coding  first of all let's take a look at how  this could be broken down in a real  program  so let's say you are interested in  genomic data genomic data can be  Illustrated with a variety of different  projects one such example is source code  two where we compare sequences of the  source of 2 virus and we study how it  developed over time accumulating  mutations that led to different types of  outcomes  as you learn about what genomic data  looks like you are then prepared to  analyze genomic data  the idea is to have enough understanding  of what that data looks like to be able  to perform analysis and interpret it  biologically  so what would be the outcome  as the outcome the student should be  able to show differences between  multiple pathogenic strains and show how  maybe those map to the protein structure  of the virus that is responsible for the  viral entry into a cell  let's say you're interested in  transcriptomic data rna-seq  then you can take a project called the  cancer genome Atlas liver cancer project  where you will again understand the  context what is cancer what is gene  expression  how to compare sets of genes that are  differentially expressed then you will  take that data set from the cancer  genome Atlas that has patient data about  many different types of cancers and run  a pipeline which will produce an  interactive dashboard that will allow  you to explore different genes Pathways  and so as the outcome the student will  identify differences between Cancer  types or between wrist cancers that or  between risk factors that lead to liver  cancer for example hepatitis infection  or alcohol consumption and explain Gene  function responsible for those  differences  the Practical side of that program is  essentially  around activities that connect different  analysis steps together in what we call  an analytical pipeline  and to identify the right set of steps  for the right kind of data that answer a  specific research question  as a result the interactive outputs make  it easy to explore different types of  visuals and elements like genes their  expression Etc  which are commonly used in Publications  that kind of explain the research that  is being done  at the end of that the student  potentially can develop an independent  research project now developing a  research project independently takes  time so the first step is preparation  and giving an example and then after the  person is comfortable  understands the analysis can work  independently and has a reasonable  project proposal they can start  developing that independent project  before they do that we have a way to  track their activity  by assigning points to different steps  that they perform  and giving them program badges when they  interact with mentors around a specific  domain that they are most interested in  now some students decide not to work on  an independent project and that's why we  provide them with curated data sets and  initial steps that can help them get  started with a project across many  different types of data for example  cancer macrophages modeling cancer  Precision medicine the tumor  microenvironment malaria infection  potato resistant drought resistance and  deliver cancer and the cancer genome  Atlas for liver cancer risk  on the other hand some students want to  develop an independent research project  those kinds of students are motivated  primarily by working on an independent  project and they need the technical  support and the tools to be able to  identify data collect it and organize it  the way that they need to and then  perform the full analysis with an  emphasis on biological interpretation  here's an example of a high school  student who has been a part of a program  designed by the science coach  organization and the outcome was that  she was able to present this research  project at a science fair when she  presents that at the science fair she is  able to demonstrate that she was able to  address a research question and  interpret the research findings that she  herself  went through and analyzed  now some students are going to be more  focused on just completing coursework  and having skills that they feel enhance  their profile for example this student  at the Georgetown University Medical  School wanted to acquire skills in  machine learning applied to biomedical  data  others could not be could could be even  faculty members like you see here  faculty members that are learning  bioinformatics for the first time or  International students that come from a  variety of backgrounds and learn  throughout the course to just establish  their understanding  now if we talk about the data science or  the skill based approach here the idea  is a little bit different  obviously the same idea of developing a  project or using data to apply their  analytical thinking applies but the  focus is more on developing coding  skills to analyze biomedical data and  the outcome would be to demonstrate that  they have proficiency they understand  the syntax they know how to write code  they know how to make the code more  efficient and then they present what  they have done using those coding skills  to a mentor that can provide them with  feedback  so a lot more emphasis here on  understanding syntax of either r or  python  and using standard packages for data  wrangling visualization statistical  analysis and machine learning  again different types of data could be  used to do that and so the choice on  what kind of data can actually dictate  what kind of exercises they will perform  at the end the student will develop a  good understanding of how to take  different parts of code and organize  them into a jupyter notebook to  understand the syntax and be able to  edit that or maybe even write code  themselves and perform coding challenges  where they can test how well they  understand what they have done and  essentially produce an application or an  analysis notebook that they can  demonstrate to others  because coding  is something that many people learn we  also recommend developing a GitHub  profile as this student from the  Georgetown biomedical uh from the  Georgetown medical school did where you  can submit a repository of developed  code and that essentially introduces you  to the community of developers and data  scientists that are on that platform and  you are using examples from the projects  that you did throughout the program to  attach that to your outcome and to your  resume as well
 

